      id: 'eng.itbasics.ipaddress',
      title: 'IP addresses, LAN and DNS',
      pre: [
        {
          q: 'The MES terminal at line 4 has address 10.20.5.40; the server it must talk to has 10.20.8.12. Both are wired into the same network cabinet. Can they reach each other just like that?',
          options: ['Yes, because they are both in the same network cabinet and so on the same switch', 'Yes, because all addresses that start with 10 together form one big network', 'Probably not without a router or firewall: different subnets', 'No, a terminal can never reach a server directly, not even in one subnet'],
          answer: 'Probably not without a router or firewall: different subnets',
          why: 'In the common layout (/24) the first three numbers must match to be in the same subnet. 5 and 8 differ, so the traffic has to go through a router or firewall.',
        },
      ],
      teach: [
        'Every device on a network has an IP address (Internet Protocol address): a number such as 10.20.5.40, four numbers from 0 to 255. It works like a house number: messages, called packets, are sent to that address.\n\nIn factories you almost always see addresses that start with 10, with 172.16 to 172.31, or with 192.168. Those are private addresses from the standard RFC 1918: they work inside the company network, not on the internet.',
        'A LAN (local area network) is the network inside one building or site: cables, switches and Wi-Fi. A switch is the box with rows of network ports that connects devices in the same LAN to each other.\n\nA subnet is a block of addresses that belong together. 10.20.5.0/24 means: the first three numbers are fixed, the last one varies. That is 256 addresses, of which 254 can be used for devices; the first and the last are reserved.',
        'A router connects subnets to each other and to the internet. If a PC in 10.20.5.0/24 wants to reach a server in 10.20.8.0/24, the traffic goes through the router: that PC’s default gateway.\n\nIn factories that router is often a firewall, or a heavy switch that also routes. When you hear “it can’t reach it”, the first question is: are they in the same subnet, and if not, is traffic between them allowed through?',
        'People don’t remember numbers. DNS (Domain Name System) is the network’s phone book: it translates a name such as historian01.plant.local into an IP address.\n\nIf an interface works on the number but not on the name, it is often a DNS problem. When a server moves to a new address, only the DNS record needs changing, as long as every system uses the name. Addresses typed by hand into machines are a classic trap when a server moves.',
        'What you see on the floor: a network cabinet with blinking switches, and sometimes a sticker with the address on a machine. A PLC (the industrial computer that controls a machine) usually has a fixed address, a static IP; laptops get one automatically from a DHCP server.\n\nSharp question for IT: “Is there an overview of which subnets the plant has and what is connected where?” The answer says a lot about how mature the network management is.',
      ],
